From b2dcf432d62c370cfaa0cc2dc30139c9706f66ac Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Arena Agent <agent@arena.ai>
Date: Sat, 10 Oct 2026 12:45:45 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 14/29] Make the loader the probe opens selectable, and stop
 testing an empty machine

The new probe tests failed on the runner and passed here, and the log the
previous commit added said exactly which case: "no loader at all". ubuntu-24.04
runners ship a system libvulkan.so.1, so that case was never testing absence, it
was testing Mesa with no ICD behind it. A test that asserts what only some
machines do is worse than no test - it fails on the machine that has nothing to
do with the app.

DD_GPU_PROBE_LOADER now names the loader to open, which makes "the loader I told
you about is not there" deterministic everywhere, and is a knob worth having by
hand when someone wants to try a probe against a particular loader build. It is
reported as a probe that could not be run, which is what the app already treats
as a failure, so a wrong name stops the session rather than waving it through.

Six cases now: pass, the device naming itself Mali-G615 MC6, a fence that never
signals, a fence that signals without doing the work, a loader named and
missing, and no driver present - the last of those asserting only that it never
reads as a pass, whatever the machine answers.

Co-authored-by: arena-agent <297053741+arena-agent@users.noreply.github.com>
---
 .../overlay/usr/local/bin/droiddeck-gpu-probe | 12 +++++++++
 tools/test/run_probe_tests.sh                 | 25 +++++++++++++++----
 2 files changed, 32 insertions(+), 5 deletions(-)

diff --git a/tools/linuxfs/overlay/usr/local/bin/droiddeck-gpu-probe b/tools/linuxfs/overlay/usr/local/bin/droiddeck-gpu-probe
index afe2ff5..33e0059 100755
--- a/tools/linuxfs/overlay/usr/local/bin/droiddeck-gpu-probe
+++ b/tools/linuxfs/overlay/usr/local/bin/droiddeck-gpu-probe
@@ -377,6 +377,18 @@ def open_loader():
     the session started with no GPU test behind it at all. Dispatching entry points is precisely
     what the loader exists to do, and it is the path the real session takes as well.
     """
+    # DD_GPU_PROBE_LOADER picks the loader to open. It exists for the test suite in tools/test,
+    # which has to be able to ask "what happens when there is no loader" on a machine where one
+    # happens to be installed, and it is useful by hand: a build of the loader can be pointed at
+    # without touching the session. A name given and not openable is an error, not a skip - being
+    # told which loader to use and not finding it is reported, not swallowed: it arrives as a
+    # probe that could not be run, which the app already treats as one that failed.
+    override = os.environ.get("DD_GPU_PROBE_LOADER", "").strip()
+    if override:
+        try:
+            return ctypes.CDLL(override, mode=os.RTLD_NOW | os.RTLD_LOCAL), override
+        except OSError as error:
+            raise Fail("DD_GPU_PROBE_LOADER=%s cannot be opened (%s)" % (override, error))
     tried = []
     for name in ("libvulkan.so.1", "libvulkan.so"):
         try:
diff --git a/tools/test/run_probe_tests.sh b/tools/test/run_probe_tests.sh
index 3fd5fcf..64d8eb6 100755
--- a/tools/test/run_probe_tests.sh
+++ b/tools/test/run_probe_tests.sh
@@ -75,12 +75,27 @@ check "the device is reported as the Mali-G615" ""                   "Mali-G615
 check "a fence that never signals blocks"       "MOCK_FENCE_RESULT=2" "DD_GPU_PROBE_SUBMIT_TIMEOUT: "  78
 check "a fence that lies about the work blocks" "MOCK_SKIP_FILL=1"     "DD_GPU_PROBE_FAILED: "          78
 
-# No loader at all: the probe must report a skip and must never block the launch.
-out=$(python3 "$WORK/probe.py" /nonexistent/icd.json 2>&1); status=$?
-if printf '%s\n' "$out" | grep -q "DD_GPU_PROBE_SKIPPED: no Vulkan loader" && [ "$status" = 0 ]; then
-    echo "  ok   no loader is a skip, and a skip never blocks"
+# A loader that was asked for by name and is not there. Deterministic, unlike "no loader at all":
+# ubuntu-24.04 runners ship a system libvulkan.so.1, so a case built on its absence passed at home
+# and did the opposite here - which is the whole reason the loader is selectable by name.
+out=$(env DD_GPU_PROBE_LOADER="$WORK/nope.so" python3 "$WORK/probe.py" /nonexistent/icd.json 2>&1); status=$?
+if printf '%s\n' "$out" | grep -q "DD_GPU_PROBE_LOADER=.*cannot be opened" && [ "$status" = 0 ]; then
+    echo "  ok   a loader named and missing is named back, as a probe that could not run"
 else
-    echo "  FAIL no loader is a skip, and a skip never blocks (exit $status)"
+    echo "  FAIL a loader named and missing is named back, as a probe that could not run (exit $status)"
+    printf '%s\n' "$out" | tail -3 | sed 's/^/       /'
+    FAILED=1
+fi
+
+# No driver manifest. On a machine with a loader the loader answers and finds nothing behind it;
+# on a machine without one the probe reports a skip. Either way it must not claim the GPU works,
+# and only those two answers are allowed - anything else is an unhandled death.
+out=$(env LD_LIBRARY_PATH="$WORK/empty" python3 "$WORK/probe.py" "$WORK/absent.json" 2>&1); status=$?
+if ! printf '%s\n' "$out" | grep -q "DD_GPU_PROBE_OK" && { [ "$status" = 0 ] || [ "$status" = 78 ]; }; then
+    echo "  ok   no driver present never reads as a pass"
+else
+    echo "  FAIL no driver present never reads as a pass (exit $status)"
+    printf '%s\n' "$out" | tail -3 | sed 's/^/       /'
     FAILED=1
 fi
 
